    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#short-answer" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Treadmill Repair: The Short Answer</a>
          <a href="#diy-repairs" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. What You Can Repair Yourself</a>
          <a href="#technician-repairs" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. What Needs a Technician</a>
          <a href="#diagnosis" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Symptom to Component: Diagnose Before You Pay</a>
          <a href="#motor-control-board" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. The Motor Control Board</a>
          <a href="#costs" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. What Treadmill Repairs Typically Cost</a>
          <a href="#finding-a-repairer" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. How to Find a Treadmill Repairer</a>
          <a href="#warranty" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Warranty Considerations Before You Repair</a>
          <a href="#repair-or-replace" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Repair or Replace: The Rule of Thumb</a>
          <a href="#before-the-visit" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Before the Technician Arrives, and Before You Order a Part</a>
          <a href="#prevention" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">11. Avoiding the Next Repair</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">12. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A treadmill is a motor, a controller, two rollers, two belts, a deck and a console, and
          nearly everything that goes wrong is one of those. Half of the list is owner-serviceable
          with ordinary tools and a little care; the other half is technician work where the part
          alone can cost a meaningful fraction of the machine. The expensive mistake is not paying
          for a repair — it is paying for the wrong one, or paying to repair a machine that was
          already worth less than the bill. This guide is about avoiding both.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Treadmill Repair: The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Treadmill repair</strong> splits cleanly into two groups. The jobs an owner can
            do with the machine unplugged, a hex key, a screwdriver and the manual: lubricating the
            deck, adjusting belt tension and tracking, replacing the safety key, cleaning the motor
            compartment, replacing motor brushes, replacing the drive belt, and — with more effort —
            replacing the walking belt. The jobs that need a technician, or at least a confident
            amateur with a multimeter and a willingness to be wrong: the drive motor, the motor
            control board, the incline motor, the console board, and wiring inside the uprights.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before paying for anything, diagnose the component. The symptom usually points at one
            or two parts, and the cheap checks — is the deck dry, is the drive belt intact, does
            the motor try to run, is there a speed signal — narrow it to one. Owners who skip that
            step pay for controllers when the fault was a dry deck, and motors when the fault was
            a drive belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            On cost, the pattern is simple. Owner-serviceable repairs are usually tens of dollars in
            parts. A technician visit for a motor or controller is usually a few hundred dollars
            all in, and on a budget machine that can approach or exceed what the treadmill is
            worth. The repair-or-replace rule that follows from that is at the end of this page,
            and our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill maintenance cost</a>
            sets the repairs in the context of what a machine costs to own over its life.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diy-repairs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. What You Can Repair Yourself</h2>
<p>
            These are the repairs that a careful owner can do without special tools or training,
            provided the machine is unplugged and the manual is to hand. They also happen to be the
            repairs that account for most treadmill problems.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Deck lubrication.</strong> The single most valuable maintenance job, and the
            cure for a large share of slipping, hesitating, overheating and noisy machines. Silicone
            lubricant, applied under the belt in the quantity the manual specifies, at the interval
            it specifies.</li>
            <li><strong>Belt tension and tracking.</strong> Two bolts at the rear of the deck,
            turned a quarter-turn at a time. Equal turns tension the belt; single-side turns steer
            it back to centre. Over-tightening is the common error.</li>
            <li><strong>The safety key.</strong> Cleaning the recess, reseating the key, replacing
            a weak or lost one with the correct part. Never bypassing it.</li>
            <li><strong>Cleaning the motor compartment.</strong> Hood off, vacuum, soft brush, keep
            the nozzle away from the board. Prevents overheating and finds other faults early.</li>
            <li><strong>Motor brushes.</strong> On brushed DC motors, the two carbon brushes are
            designed to be replaced. Screw caps or clips, correct part for the motor, run in at low
            speed afterwards. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-motor-brushes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill motor brushes</a>
            covers inspection and replacement.</li>
            <li><strong>The drive belt.</strong> The short ribbed belt from motor to front roller.
            Replacing it means slackening the motor mount, fitting the new belt and resetting the
            tension; it is fiddly but not difficult. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-drive-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">drive belt guide</a>
            explains how to check it and when it is worn.</li>
            <li><strong>The walking belt.</strong> The largest owner job: removing the motor hood,
            the side rails, the rear roller and often the front roller, sliding the old belt off and
            the new one on, then reassembling, tensioning and tracking. A couple of hours for a
            first-timer. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-replace-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to replace a treadmill belt</a>
            walks through it, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-replacement-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt replacement cost guide</a>
            compares doing it yourself with having it done.</li>
            <li><strong>The speed sensor.</strong> A small magnet-and-sensor pair on the front
            roller. Checking the magnet, the gap and the wiring is simple; replacing the sensor is
            cheap and usually straightforward.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            A general rule for all of these: if the manual shows the procedure, it is intended to
            be done by the owner. If it does not, and the only guidance is from forums, be more
            cautious, particularly where the warranty is concerned.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="technician-repairs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What Needs a Technician</h2>
<p>
            These repairs involve mains voltage, model-specific electronics, heavy components or
            calibration, and the consequences of getting them wrong range from a wasted part to a
            machine that is unsafe. Most owners should have them done, or at least diagnosed, by
            someone who does it regularly.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The drive motor.</strong> Heavy, expensive, and only worth replacing after
            the brushes, commutator, drive belt and controller have been ruled out. The new motor
            must match the controller's voltage and the machine's mounting, and some controllers
            need calibrating to a new motor.</li>
            <li><strong>The motor control board (MCB).</strong> The board under the hood that
            drives the motor. Model-specific, sensitive to being connected wrongly, and the part
            most often replaced unnecessarily. The next section is about it.</li>
            <li><strong>The incline motor.</strong> A linear actuator under the deck or at the
            front of the frame. Replacing it means supporting the deck, swapping the actuator and
            then recalibrating the incline; on some machines the motor also has to be set to a
            specific extension before fitting.</li>
            <li><strong>The console board.</strong> The electronics behind the display. Often as
            expensive as the controller, sometimes more on touchscreen consoles, and frequently
            blamed for faults that are actually the cable or the controller below.</li>
            <li><strong>Wiring in the uprights.</strong> The cable from the base to the console
            runs inside an upright and flexes every time a folding machine is folded. Finding and
            replacing a broken conductor means partially dismantling the frame.</li>
            <li><strong>Roller bearings.</strong> Pressing bearings out of a roller and new ones in
            is a workshop job; most technicians replace the roller as a unit.</li>
            <li><strong>Deck replacement.</strong> Possible for an owner with the right part, but
            heavy and involved; our
            <a href="/treadmill-deck-replacement/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">deck replacement guide</a>
            covers when it is worth doing at all.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The line is not absolute. A confident owner with a multimeter and the service manual
            can replace a controller; a nervous one should not replace a drive belt. The question
            to ask is whether you can confirm the part is faulty before buying it, fit it without
            guessing at connections, and test the result safely. If the answer to any of those is
            no, it is a technician job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diagnosis" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Symptom to Component: Diagnose Before You Pay</h2>
<p>
            Every treadmill repair should begin with the question "which part?", and the symptom
            answers it more often than owners expect. The table gives the usual suspects for each
            common symptom, in order of likelihood, with the check that separates them. Everything
            in the check column is done with the machine unplugged unless it says otherwise.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Symptom</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Usual suspects, in order</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">The check that separates them</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Completely dead, no lights</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Outlet, cord, reset breaker, safety key, console cable, controller</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lamp in the outlet; power switch light; reseat console cable</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Console lit, belt will not move, motor silent</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Safety key, controller fuse or board, motor connector</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Key reseated; listen for relay click on start; look for fuse and scorch marks</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor spins, belt does not move</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Drive belt slipped, snapped or glazed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hood off; turn front roller by hand, watch motor pulley</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt runs a few seconds then stops with error</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Speed sensor or magnet, sensor wiring, controller input</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Magnet present on roller; gap; continuity on sensor</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt hesitates under foot</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Dry deck, loose belt, worn drive belt, worn belt or deck</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Feel deck under belt; lift test; inspect drive belt</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt surges or hunts on its own</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Speed sensor signal, controller</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stop using; sensor checks first, then controller</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Runs hot, shuts down after a set time, restarts when cool</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Dry deck, tight belt, dust, brushes, overload, supply</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lubricate; lift test; vacuum; inspect brushes; direct to wall outlet</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Burning smell, hot electrics</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Controller, motor windings, wiring</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stop and unplug; inspect board for scorching; technician</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Breaker trips on start</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Controller, motor short, wiring</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stop; technician with a meter</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor crackles, loses power, smells of ozone</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Worn brushes, scored commutator</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Remove brush caps; measure brush length; look at commutator</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline will not move, or reads wrong</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Calibration, incline motor connector, incline motor, position sensor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Run calibration; reseat connector; listen for actuator</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Display dead, buttons work or machine runs</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Console cable, console board</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Reseat cable at both ends; wiggle test with power on</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Thump once per belt revolution</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt seam or wear, roller flat spot</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Mark belt; see whether thump tracks the mark</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Squeak or grinding from front or rear</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Roller bearing, dry deck, loose frame bolt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lubricate; spin rollers by hand; tighten frame bolts</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Two things to take from the table. First, a dry deck appears against half the symptoms
            on it, which is why lubrication is the first step in almost any repair. Second, the
            controller is on the list for many symptoms but is rarely the most likely, which is why
            the cheap checks come first. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill troubleshooting guide</a>
            works through each symptom in more detail, and the brand-specific guides on the site
            cover console behaviour that differs by manufacturer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor-control-board" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. The Motor Control Board</h2>
<p>
            The <strong>motor control board</strong> — MCB, controller, motor controller, lower
            board, depending on the brand — deserves a section of its own because it is the
            component owners search for most, the one most often replaced on a guess, and the one
            where the guess costs the most.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What it does</h3>
          <p>
            It sits under the motor hood and takes mains power in, converts it to the voltage the
            motor needs, and regulates how much of that reaches the motor from moment to moment.
            It reads the speed sensor to hold the belt at the set speed, drives the incline motor on
            most designs, talks to the console up the upright cable, and carries the fuse and
            relays that protect the rest. It is the part that does the work the console asks for.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Symptoms when it fails</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Console lit and key in, but the motor makes no attempt to run.</li>
            <li>The belt surges, pulses or runs at the wrong speed with a good speed sensor.</li>
            <li>A blown fuse on the board, particularly one that blows again when replaced.</li>
            <li>Scorch marks, discoloured components, a bulging or leaking capacitor, or a burnt
            smell from under the hood.</li>
            <li>The breaker trips the moment the machine starts.</li>
            <li>The incline and the belt both misbehave, or the console reports a communication
            error with the base.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why it fails</h3>
          <p>
            Four causes account for most of it. <strong>Power surges</strong>, from storms or from
            the supply, which is the argument for a
            <a href="/treadmill-surge-protector/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">surge protector on a treadmill</a>.
            <strong>A dry deck</strong>, which raises the motor current the board has to deliver,
            session after session, until a component gives out — the most common cause on older
            machines, and the one that is entirely preventable. <strong>Dust</strong>, which blankets
            the heat sink and keeps the board hot. And <strong>heat</strong> generally: a machine
            in a warm room, used hard, with blocked vents. Age does the rest; electrolytic
            capacitors dry out over years regardless.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Replacing it</h3>
          <p>
            The exact board is model-specific. Boards that look identical can be configured
            differently for motor voltage, speed range and incline, and a board from a similar
            model may run the motor at the wrong speed or not at all. Order by model and serial
            number, from the manufacturer or a supplier who confirms the fit. Before fitting a new
            board, find and fix whatever killed the old one, or the new one goes the same way:
            lubricate the deck, clean the compartment, check the motor is not drawing excessive
            current because of worn brushes or a seized bearing, and put a surge protector on the
            supply. Some replacement boards need calibration to the machine after fitting; the
            instructions with the part or the service manual will say.
          </p>
          <p>
            Do not condemn the board until the cheaper suspects are cleared: safety key, reset
            breaker, drive belt, speed sensor and magnet, motor connector, and the motor itself
            turning freely by hand. A board replaced on a guess that turns out to have been a speed
            sensor is the single most expensive common mistake in treadmill repair.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. What Treadmill Repairs Typically Cost</h2>
<p>
            The figures below are labelled estimates for home treadmills in the United States —
            "in the region of" — and they are not quotes. Parts prices vary a great deal by brand
            and model; a controller for a premium machine can cost several times what the same
            part costs for a budget one. Labour varies by area and by whether the technician is
            manufacturer-authorised or independent. A technician visit usually has two parts: a
            call-out or diagnostic fee for coming to the house, typically in the region of $75 to
            $150, and labour on top at an hourly rate, with the part on top of that. Some repairers
            fold the call-out into the labour; ask.
