        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Diagnose the component before anyone is paid.</strong> Lubricate
            the deck, check the drive belt, the safety key and the speed sensor, and confirm the
            motor tries to run; most faults are owner-serviceable and most of the rest narrow to one
            part with those checks.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Then weigh the all-in cost against what the machine is worth
            today.</strong> Tens of dollars for a DIY part is nearly always worth it; several
            hundred for a motor or controller is worth it on a sound mid-range or premium machine
            and rarely on a budget one. If the warranty might apply, make that call first — our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">warranty guide</a>
            explains what is usually covered.
          </p>
